[Music]  welcome back to cbs this morning  saturday the aftermath of a tragic day  continues to haunt the city of  philadelphia  36 years ago on may 13 1985  a year's long confrontation between the  city and a black liberation organization  known as move  ended with a bombing killing nearly a  dozen people  including children now it's a merge of  the remains of some of the victims were  never returned to their families  and may have been mistreated family  members speak out about their initial  loss  and the fresh pain that's now followed  where did you live lived at 6221. see  this door here and then you see the  older door and you see the sign  how long has it been since you've been  here since i've been back to this street  i come back to this street every year  you know all of that stuff that  move was talking about police brutality  all those things are real  every day i see those kids  there's not a day that goes by where i  don't like  think about them in some way not one  mike africa junior lost his playmates  his home  and his innocence on this street 36  years ago  [Music]  it was mother's day 1985. the day  philadelphia police  dropped a bomb on three blocks of this  community  home to a group of counter culture  activists known as the move  organization i wasn't here that day  i was at my grandmother's house which is  about four miles from here  you saw the smoke it was black and you  could see like ashes  floating around the blocks were  decimated  61 homes destroyed and 11 people killed  five of them children this is  a really hard thing for me to talk about  because i feel like  i am reliving 1985.  nearly four decades later the parents of  those young victims  learned their nightmare had not ended  we are in pain we're not weak don't  misunderstand  we are not weak by no means we are very  strong thanks to the power down after  that pain triggered by a shocking  revelation  concerning their children's remains the  penn museum  the university of pennsylvania and  princeton university  had used the bones of 14 year old tree  africa  and 12 year old delicia africa for  research  and teaching without the knowledge or  consent of their family  each university apologizing for its  actions  i was in shock i'm still in shock i  couldn't believe it as far as i knew  everybody was buried but they weren't  a second set of remains thought to be  cremated four years ago  were found in a box in the medical  examiner's office  both penn and the city of philadelphia  had to be held accountable for what  happened these folks were black  these folks had dreadlocks these folks  were anti-social  so we they've done all the labeling it  takes to marginalize  them make them be less than a human  being right and so  it becomes easy to be dismissive about  them  move practice the first amendment the  right to assembly  walter palmer is an activist a lawyer  and professor of american racism at the  university of pennsylvania  nobody wanted to recognize move for  sickly city palmer was witness to the  tensions between police and move  the first police sweep through the house  was fruitless move members had hidden in  a basement  including during this 1978 standoff  when officers tried to evict members  following noise and sanitation  complaints from neighbors  an hour later as fire hoses flooded the  basement several shots rang out  answered by a barrage of police gunfire  i get caught in crossfire because the  police open up  on move i saw six policemen empty their  sub thompson machine guns  police officer james ramp was killed  during the confrontation  there's still debate over whether or not  he was the victim of friendly fire  nine move members were convicted of  third degree murder  and sentenced to more than 30 years in  prison  including mike africa junior's parents  mike  and debbie africa his mother gave birth  to him  in prison seven years later came the  horrific  firestorm leaving hundreds of people  displaced  it doesn't look anything like it did it  doesn't feel the same or anything it  really doesn't it's this whole block  the whole thing has been recast  yeah because we used to go off trail  because everybody else was on trail  lisa kate's family was not a part of  move  but their home was destroyed in the  mayhem  it was just something that you know  shouldn't have happened  really shouldn't have happened a federal  jury  found that philadelphia authorities used  excessive force against the group  and ordered the city to pay 1.5 million  dollars in damages in 1996.  i'm not hurt i'm disappointed i'm glad  it's finally over  this ain't about money this is about  taking a stand  for all people so that this government  knows  that the people ain't going to have them  bombing people  and burning people alive mayor jim  kinney  has formally apologized on behalf of the  city for the continued pain  and mishandling of the remains of the 11  victims  this is about the families this is about  the deceased this is about the children  that were never treated respectfully and  decently in 36 years  we are going to stay on the move and we  are going to push for accountability and  we are not going to stop  until we get it but mike africa is among  those  calling for an independent investigation  we believe that apology without action  is meaningless what will an  investigation bring to light that you  don't already know  i think the investigation will bring to  light  the severity of  the crime you know it's not just about a  bomb that was dropped a fire that  happened and 11 people died  it's also about a cover-up it's what we  hope to  expose and deal with  and mike africa junior says the remains  of those victims have still not been  returned to their families  after 40 years in prison he worked very  very hard to help get his parents out  there  on parole now for the crime that they  say he  that they committed and killing that  police officer 36 years later it's still  stunning to look at that file video  of that bomb drop in that neighborhood  it's hard to believe isn't it  it is michelle thank you

